WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:21.460
Andrew Carnegie: Robber Baron or America’s
Greatest Philanthropist?

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.449
When it comes to judging a person by their
legacy, things are seldom black or white.

00:00:26.449 --> 00:00:31.859
In other words, few people can be considered
purely good or unequivocally evil.

00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:36.840
Andrew Carnegie was a man of paradoxes. He
was a businessman, first and foremost, and,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.000
oftentimes, this meant that someone else had
to pay the price when he made his fortunes.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.120
Although publicly he championed the welfare
of his employees, the working conditions in

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:51.000
his many factories and plants didn’t exactly
match up with his touted values.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.399
On the other hand, Carnegie donated almost
his entire wealth to humanitarian causes,

00:00:55.399 --> 00:01:01.100
not to mention the fact that his actions set
the trend for all the other billionaire philanthropists

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.520
who followed. Therefore, it is up to you to
decide where exactly his legacy sits as today

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:12.670
we take a look at the life of industrialist
Andrew Carnegie.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:18.820
Early Years
Right off the bat, we should have a quick

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:23.740
talk about Andrew Carnegie’s last name and
the fact that most people pronounce it wrong.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:28.049
As a spokeswoman for the Carnegie Corporation
of New York specifies, he was Scottish so

00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:33.390
the correct pronunciation of his name is car-NAY-gee,
not CAR-ne-gee, as most people say it.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.950
Now that we got that out of the way, let’s
look at Carnegie’s early years. He was born

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:43.450
on November 25, 1835, in the town of Dunfermline
in the Scottish county of Fife, to William

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.909
and Margaret Carnegie. He had a younger brother
named Thomas who would go on to play a vital

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.670
role in the family’s business interests
until he died of pneumonia at just 43 years

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:53.670
of age.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.180
Carnegie had a modest upbringing as his father
was a hand-loom weaver. His first home consisted

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:03.000
of just one main room inside a cottage which
the Carnegies shared with another family.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.080
At the time of Andrew’s birth, Dunfermline
was quite famous for its production of high-quality

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.860
linen. This created a high demand for Will
Carnegie’s services which allowed the family

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.460
a brief respite from poverty, enough that
they were able to move to a larger house.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:20.751
This didn’t last long, though, and, in fact,
things soon became worse than ever. The Industrial

00:02:20.751 --> 00:02:25.500
Revolution brought with it steam-powered looms
which made hand weavers like Will Carnegie

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.560
obsolete.
During the 1840s, Andrew’s father joined

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.980
Chartism, a political movement which sought
to improve conditions for the working class.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:38.360
The main goal of Chartists was to obtain universal
manhood suffrage - all men would have the

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.910
right to vote which, theoretically, would
allow the masses to take over the government

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:46.400
from the aristocracy. Will and his father-in-law,
Thomas Morrison, actually became quite prominent

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.330
members and were heads of the Chartist chapter
in Dunfermline. Regardless, the movement was

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:55.670
struck down in Parliament and, eventually,
faded away. It makes it even stranger that

00:02:55.670 --> 00:03:00.540
Carnegie would go on to crush unionizing efforts
later in life given that his own father campaigned

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.750
hard for better worker conditions.
In 1848, there were few prospects left for

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:09.271
the Carnegie family in Scotland. Margaret
Carnegie heard tales of a better life in America

00:03:09.271 --> 00:03:14.140
from her sister who already moved there. The
family sold all their possessions and secured

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:19.360
a loan to book passage on a ship from Glasgow
to New York City. It was a long, miserable

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.330
trip where the poor passengers were packed
in the cargo hold like sardines. Those who

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:29.010
did not become sick from the terrible conditions
had to work on deck as the ship’s crew was

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.680
undermanned.
But the voyage ultimately ended successfully.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.660
There was no immigration station on Ellis
Island yet, so the ship disembarked somewhere

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:41.240
in lower Manhattan and the Carnegies continued
their trip west until they reached their final

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:48.890
destination of Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.
Life in America

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:55.100
The Carnegie family settled in Allegheny City,
a municipality next to Pittsburgh which would

00:03:55.100 --> 00:04:00.030
get annexed a few decades later. Thirteen-year-old
Andrew needed a job and he found one as a

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:04.820
bobbin boy at the same cotton mill where his
father was employed. He had to work from sunup

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:10.150
to sundown and made just one dollar and twenty
cents per week, but Carnegie later said in

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:14.650
his autobiography that none of the millions
he made later “gave [him] such happiness

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.890
as [his] first week’s earnings.” He was
now a breadwinner for the family.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:23.320
Carnegie soon moved on to a new job at a bobbins
manufacturer where he made two dollars a week.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.540
He then became the boss’s clerk because
he had nice handwriting. Later, his uncle

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:31.890
found him a better job as a messenger boy
for the telegraph service which paid two dollars

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.520
fifty per week.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.070
Working as a messenger allowed Carnegie to
meet many of Pittsburgh’s most influential

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:42.110
people who often received urgent news from
outside the city. Andrew excelled at his job

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:47.520
so he was promoted to telegraph operator a
few years later. In 1853, Carnegie made another

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.450
career switch and went to work as a clerk
and operator for Thomas A. Scott who was then

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:57.230
a superintendent with Pennsylvania Railroad
but will later become the president of the

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:01.290
entire company.
The next few years saw ups and downs for Carnegie.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:06.090
His father passed away but, in 1859, he was
promoted to superintendent when his mentor,

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:09.730
Thomas Scott, became vice-president of the
Pennsylvania Railroad Company. He now had

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:15.650
a salary of $1,500 a year. Scott clearly took
a liking to Carnegie who he referred to as

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:20.480
“my boy Andy” and not only taught him
the ins and outs of business, but also enabled

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.130
him to make some serious money for the first
time.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:28.020
One day, Scott approached Carnegie and asked
him if he had $500 to buy ten shares of Adams

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:32.000
Express stock because it would provide him
with a nice return. This information was,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.170
of course, obtained through insider trading,
but they weren’t that fussed about it back

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:40.540
then. Carnegie didn’t have anywhere near
that sum, but his mother mortgaged the family

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:44.740
home and he made his first investment. Soon
afterwards, he received an envelope addressed

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:49.350
to “Andrew Carnegie, Esquire” from the
Gold Exchange Bank of New York. It contained

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.290
a check for ten dollars, representing the
monthly dividend on his investment. It was

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:58.010
the first time that Carnegie made any money
without having to work manual labor for it.

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:02.050
He saw that check as the “goose that lays
the golden eggs.”

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:05.970
Carnegie became involved in more business
ventures, most of them railroad-related. He

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:10.520
met with Theodore T. Woodruff, the man who
invented the sleeping car. He was impressed

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:14.920
with the design and brought it to the attention
of Scott and the Pennsylvania Railroad Company’s

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:19.170
president, John Edgar Thomson. As a reward,
Woodruff offered him an eighth interest in

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:26.242
the venture.
Business during the Civil War

00:06:26.242 --> 00:06:30.530
During the American Civil War, Thomas Scott
was first appointed as Colonel of Volunteers

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:35.340
and then Assistant Secretary of War. Carnegie,
of course, was right there behind him as his

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:39.670
second-in-command. His main duty was to ensure
that the railroads and telegraph system were

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:42.360
maintained in working order for the Union
Army.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:46.389
At the outset of the war, Carnegie took a
trip to inspect the main telegraph line to

00:06:46.389 --> 00:06:50.340
Washington. At some distance from the capital,
he noticed a few wires which had been pinned

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:54.300
down to the ground using wooden stakes. He
went to remove the stakes, not realizing that

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:58.660
the wires had been pulled to one side prior
to being pinned and were taut. They flung

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:03.780
upwards violently, hitting Carnegie in the
face and cutting a huge gash on his cheek.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:07.240
He later joked that he was among the first
to “shed [his] blood for [his] country.”

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:12.050
During this time, Carnegie got the opportunity
to meet President Abraham Lincoln and many

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:16.320
members of his cabinet. He later wrote with
great esteem about Lincoln whom he considered

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.580
an “extraordinary man.” He mentioned that
the president always had a kind word for everybody,

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:25.160
even the youngest messenger boys. Carnegie
described Lincoln as one of the “most homely

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:30.580
men” he ever saw when his features were
in repose, but that when animated, “intellect

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:34.530
shone through his eyes...to a degree...never
seen in any other.”

00:07:34.530 --> 00:08:07.040
A Man of Industry

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:27.360
Just because he

00:08:27.360 --> 00:09:01.380
was

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.130
involved with the war did not mean that Carnegie
stopped looking for new business ventures.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:09.250
If anything, this new experience revealed
to him the industries which he believed would

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:13.480
flourish in the years and decades to come.
First, he invested in an oil company at the

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:17.750
right time and this quickly yielded huge returns
for him which provided Carnegie the capital

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:21.330
needed to start his own enterprises.
The up-and-coming industrialist correctly

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:26.040
predicted that the demand for railroads would
only increase and he wanted to be there to

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:31.740
meet those demands. First, in 1864, he founded
the Superior Rail Mill and Blast Furnaces,

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:36.960
a company that manufactured rails. Soon afterwards
came the Pittsburgh Locomotive Works which

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.590
built, you guessed it, locomotives.
Even though Carnegie had a high-paying job

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:45.480
with the Pennsylvania Railroad Company and
the favor of his superiors, he decided it

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:50.560
was time to leave after the war and strike
it out on his own. He remained on good terms

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:54.260
with them, though, and secured many lucrative
contracts in the years to follow.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:59.310
In 1865, Andrew Carnegie founded the Keystone
Bridge Company, with his brother Thomas serving

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:04.490
as treasurer. It was followed by several ironworks
and plants. Steel was the way of the future

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:10.360
in Carnegie’s mind and, indeed, would become
responsible for the bulk of his vast fortune.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:14.800
Again having a keen attentiveness for the
future, Carnegie was an early adopter of the

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:20.870
Bessemer process, a new way of refining steel
from molten pig iron. It was cheap and efficient

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:25.880
and allowed for the mass production of steel
on a scale never seen before. Carnegie invested

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:30.050
heavily in this technique and it paid off
big for him as he became the dominant force

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:34.600
in the American steel industry. He also prioritized
vertical integration to make sure that he

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:39.400
controlled companies responsible for all aspects
of his steel business such as supplying raw

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:48.060
materials and transporting finished goods.
The Billion Dollar Corporation

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:52.399
The steel business made Andrew Carnegie one
of the richest men in the world. Of course,

00:10:52.399 --> 00:10:57.490
there were others who looked on at his steel
empire with covetous eyes. One of those men

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:03.990
was financier and banker John Pierpont Morgan.
In 1898, he bought and merged several steel

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:09.210
producers to form the Federal Steel Company
which, by 1900, became America’s second-largest

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:12.620
manufacturer of steel (behind Carnegie’s
company, of course).

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:17.320
But second place was not good enough for J.
P. Morgan. A war was brewing between the two

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:23.020
corporations and Carnegie knew it. The Scotsman
believed he could weather the storm and successfully

00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:27.210
drive off the competition. On the other hand,
Carnegie was not a young man anymore, and

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:29.950
had already been contemplating retirement
for a while now.

00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:35.230
The lynchpin in this business venture was
Charles M. Schwab, a high-ranking executive

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:40.240
and trusted advisor of Carnegie. He was first
approached by Morgan who preferred an alliance

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:44.890
over a war and then convinced Carnegie to
accept a buyout for his company. The asking

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:51.690
price was $480 million for Carnegie Steel,
a term which Morgan accepted on the spot with

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:57.050
no negotiation. Later, while talking to Carnegie,
the financier half-jokingly said he would

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:02.190
have paid another $100 million.
The deal was done in 1901 and Carnegie personally

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:07.130
gained around $230 million from the sale of
his company. J. P. Morgan went on to buy several

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:11.940
other businesses and merged them all together
to form U.S. Steel, the first billion-dollar

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:19.060
corporation in the world.
Personal High Points and Heartbreaks

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:24.410
Carnegie’s career might have been full of
twists and turns, but his personal life was

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:27.720
decidedly less exciting. He was a bachelor
for most of his life, allegedly due to his

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:32.440
relationship with his mother to whom he was
completely devoted. When Carnegie moved his

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:35.709
headquarters to New York City, the two shared
a suite at the Windsor Hotel.

00:12:35.709 --> 00:12:40.640
Even so, during the 1880s, Carnegie made the
acquaintance of Louise Whitfield, a woman

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:44.899
who was 21 years younger than him. The industrialist
noted in his autobiography that, the more

00:12:44.899 --> 00:12:49.649
time they spent together, the more he realized
that Louise was “the perfect one beyond

00:12:49.649 --> 00:12:54.980
any [he] had met” and all other young women
he knew “faded into ordinary beings.”

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:59.260
Curiously, Carnegie believed that his vast
fortune worked against him as Louise thought

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:03.600
he already had everything in the world while
she would have preferred to be the companion

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:08.560
and constant supporter of a struggling man,
mimicking the relationship of her parents.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:13.170
The year 1886 proved to be devastating for
Carnegie. Both his mother and his brother

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:17.640
died within days of each other, while Carnegie
himself was struggling at death’s door with

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:22.930
typhoid fever. Eventually, he managed to recover
and Louise’s company became the only “ray

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:27.670
of hope and comfort” in his life. She now
realized that Carnegie needed her more than

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:32.410
any other man and she became his “helpmeet”
as he described her. The two married on April

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:39.940
22, 1887, and went on to have only one child
- a girl named Margaret born ten years later.

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:47.170
The Johnstown Flood
As is usually the case with wealthy businessmen,

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:51.700
Andrew Carnegie’s life was not devoid of
controversies. After his brother Thomas died,

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:56.481
many of his duties were taken on by Henry
Clay Frick, another industrialist who first

00:13:56.481 --> 00:14:01.350
founded his own coke manufacturing company
and later became chairman of Carnegie Steel.

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:06.230
It seems that Frick was at the center of all
the major scandals which tainted Carnegie’s

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:10.370
reputation. First was the time when he and
his associates caused one of the deadliest

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:12.269
floods in American history.

00:14:12.269 --> 00:14:16.600
During the early 1880s, Frick founded the
South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club. It counted

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:22.100
between 50 and 60 members who included industrialists,
bankers, attorneys, and almost all other prominent

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:25.529
men in Pennsylvania. Unsurprisingly, Andrew
Carnegie was one of them.

00:14:25.529 --> 00:14:30.180
At first, the club seemed rather innocuous.
It was just another place where rich guys

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:33.930
could relax, smoke some cigars and maybe do
some fishing. However, they wanted their own

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:38.700
private place where they would not be disturbed.
To that end, they purchased the South Fork

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:41.300
Dam on Lake Conemaugh.
The dam was originally built by the Commonwealth

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:46.010
of Pennsylvania a few decades prior, but it
had since been abandoned and switched ownership

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:50.339
multiple times. When the South Fork Club made
the purchase, it was still functional, but

00:14:50.339 --> 00:14:55.490
in a state of disrepair due to neglect and
the fact that a previous owner removed parts

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:58.760
to sell for scrap metal.
The club arranged for modifications to the

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:02.860
dam, but they were more concerned with making
it suit their needs rather than making it

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:07.200
safe - they added a fish screen to the spillway,
they leveled off the top to make room for

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:11.500
a road and, most importantly, they lowered
the dam by several feet. Whenever the dam

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:14.959
sprung a leak, it was quickly patched up and
forgotten about.

00:15:14.959 --> 00:15:20.740
It was just a matter of time before disaster
struck. That moment happened on May 31, 1889.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:25.720
The area had been hit by one of the most powerful
storms ever recorded in the region which saw

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:31.320
between 6 and 10 inches of rainfall in 24
hours. That morning, the club manager saw

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:35.510
that the lake had swollen and was ready to
tip over the dam. He gathered some workers

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:40.180
in a desperate attempt to unclog the spillway
which had been blocked by debris caught in

00:15:40.180 --> 00:15:45.240
the fish screen. Their efforts proved in vain.
Shortly before 3 p.m., the dam breached, sending

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:49.899
over 3.8 billion gallons of water to the towns
below.

00:15:49.899 --> 00:15:54.241
South Fork was the first hit, but got off
easy because it was on high ground. Several

00:15:54.241 --> 00:15:59.279
towns were affected, but Johnstown was completely
devastated in what was, at the time, the single

00:15:59.279 --> 00:16:04.390
greatest loss of life in American history.
According to the Johnstown Area Heritage Association,

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:10.840
2,209 people were killed, including 400 children.
Their bodies were washed away as far as Cincinnati,

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:14.140
400 miles away.
For the South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club,

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:18.620
the main priority was negating any liability
on their part. They used all the money and

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:23.089
influence at their disposal and ensured that
none of them were ever held accountable by

00:16:23.089 --> 00:16:27.540
successfully arguing that it was an “act
of God.” People tried to sue them, but they

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:30.330
all lost in court.
We can’t say with any certainty how much

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:35.410
of the blame should be put on Carnegie personally.
He was simply the most prominent member of

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:40.029
the club, but that doesn’t mean he was involved
with the maintenance of the dam. Even so,

00:16:40.029 --> 00:16:43.760
he, like all the other members, agreed to
sweep the whole thing under the rug and made

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:47.630
no further mention of the flood or the club,
not even in his own autobiography.

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:52.760
A recent study from the University of Pittsburgh
at Johnstown confirmed what many suspected

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:57.390
all along - it was the modifications made
by the club which caused the dam to fail,

00:16:57.390 --> 00:17:02.279
not the heavy rainfall.
The Homestead Strike

00:17:02.279 --> 00:17:08.980
Throughout his career, Carnegie liked to portray
himself as a fair employer who looked after

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:13.449
the rights and wellbeing of his workers, but
he didn’t always practice what he preached.

00:17:13.449 --> 00:17:18.589
At no time was this more evident than in 1892
during the Homestead strike.

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:22.759
On one side, we had the Homestead Steel Works,
part of Carnegie’s steel empire. On the

00:17:22.759 --> 00:17:27.689
other, there was the Amalgamated Association
of Iron and Steel Workers or AA, the most

00:17:27.689 --> 00:17:31.549
powerful labor union at the time.
The workers organized a strike after their

00:17:31.549 --> 00:17:36.741
last deal ended to negotiate a wage increase.
Henry Clay Frick, who was in charge of operations

00:17:36.741 --> 00:17:42.210
at the plant, wanted the exact opposite - he
intended to slash their wages and, more importantly,

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:45.690
break up the union.
The situation unfolded exactly like many other

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:50.269
labor disputes of that time. The union went
on strike. The company brought in non-union

00:17:50.269 --> 00:17:56.169
workers and called in over three hundred Pinkerton
agents who acted as muscle. Eventually, things

00:17:56.169 --> 00:18:00.909
escalated to violence and around ten men were
killed and dozens more injured. In the end,

00:18:00.909 --> 00:18:04.470
the state militia intervened and the town
was placed under martial law.

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:09.289
The situation took an unexpected turn on July
23 when a prominent anarchist named Alexander

00:18:09.289 --> 00:18:14.470
Berkman tried to assassinate Henry Frick in
his office in downtown Pittsburgh. Berkman

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:18.880
shot Frick twice and then stabbed him in the
legs several times. Despite this, Frick survived

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:22.470
and Berkman was arrested.
Although the anarchist had no ties to the

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:27.480
AA, his actions generated sympathy for Frick
and turned public opinion against the union.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:31.909
Consequently, other employers refused to hire
AA workers. Their membership numbers collapsed

00:18:31.909 --> 00:18:36.859
and their funds dried out. The strike ended
with a huge victory for the industrialists.

00:18:36.859 --> 00:18:41.820
Like with the Johnstown flood, it is a matter
of debate how much blame can be placed on

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:45.899
Carnegie. He was in Scotland during the most
violent days of the strike. In his autobiography,

00:18:45.899 --> 00:18:50.270
he claimed ignorance of the matter, saying
that nothing else “wounded [him] so deeply”

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:54.649
and calling it the biggest regret of his business
career. However, other documents suggest that

00:18:54.649 --> 00:18:59.309
he was well-aware of Frick’s actions and
gave him approval to do whatever was necessary,

00:18:59.309 --> 00:19:04.090
writing him in a letter that “we are with
you to the end.”

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:11.509
Philanthropy
“The man who dies rich, dies disgraced.”

00:19:11.509 --> 00:19:16.169
Those were the words of Andrew Carnegie, expressing
his belief that it was the responsibility

00:19:16.169 --> 00:19:20.279
of the wealthy to use all their extra money
for the betterment of society. He made this

00:19:20.279 --> 00:19:24.650
clear in 1889 when he published an article
titled “The Gospel of Wealth”, where he

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:29.820
outlined his views. Carnegie was against simply
passing down the fortune to heirs because,

00:19:29.820 --> 00:19:33.159
in his experience, these heirs were often
self-indulgent or irresponsible.

00:19:33.159 --> 00:19:37.429
In this regard, the Scottish industrialist
put his money where his mouth was. By the

00:19:37.429 --> 00:19:42.649
time he died in 1919 of bronchial pneumonia,
the 83-year-old businessman had given away

00:19:42.649 --> 00:19:48.759
over $350 million of his personal fortune.
He was still a rich man when he died so, technically,

00:19:48.759 --> 00:19:52.590
according to his own words, he died disgraced,
but he made provisions so that the rest of

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:57.179
his wealth would go to worthy causes. His
family kept the property and a small trust

00:19:57.179 --> 00:20:01.119
to live comfortably, but other than that,
everything else was given away.

00:20:01.119 --> 00:20:05.770
As a self-made man, Carnegie wasn’t a big
fan of charities for two reasons. First, he

00:20:05.770 --> 00:20:09.539
did not trust that those charities would manage
his money well and, second, he believed that

00:20:09.539 --> 00:20:14.070
by just giving money directly to people, you
did not enable them to actually improve their

00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:17.389
station in life.
Carnegie’s biggest passion was education.

00:20:17.389 --> 00:20:22.659
He set up 3,000 public libraries in cities
all over the United States, Britain, and Canada,

00:20:22.659 --> 00:20:27.759
starting in 1883 with his hometown of Dunfermline.
Many other institutions received generous

00:20:27.759 --> 00:20:32.470
grants such as various trusts and universities.
The Carnegie Corporation of New York received

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:38.669
the largest gift of $125 million and they
still do philanthropic work in Carnegie’s

00:20:38.669 --> 00:20:43.650
name today.
Do all these donations make up for Carnegie’s

00:20:43.650 --> 00:20:49.990
plutocratic policies? Is that why he did it
in the first place, to help ease his conscience?

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:53.950
These are questions we couldn’t possibly
answer, but we do know that the actions of

00:20:53.950 --> 00:21:01.450
Andrew Carnegie set him apart and made him
unique among the ruthless industrialists of

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:15.519
the 19th century.

